.TH C-ARBINT 3 "@MAN_DATE@" "c-arbint @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
c-arbint \- portable arbitrary-precision integer library for C
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <arbint.h>
.fi
.PP
Link with:
.PP
.nf
.B cc ... $(pkg-config --cflags --libs c-arbint)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B c-arbint
provides signed arbitrary-precision integer arithmetic with explicit
allocator-aware object management and runtime performance optimization.
.PP
This page documents the functions currently exported by
.BR libarbint
(version 0.0.1).
Additional declarations may exist in
.BR arbint.h
for future releases.
.PP
The library includes optimized code paths for x86 processors supporting
BMI2 (multiplication), AVX2 (doubling), POPCNT (bit counting), SHA-NI
(hashing), and SSE4.2 (CRC32C hashing) instructions, and for AArch64
processors supporting
CRC32 and PMULL instructions (CRC32C hashing), with automatic runtime
dispatch based on CPU feature detection.
.SH TYPES
.TP
.B arbint_t
Opaque integer object type (single-object array form). Initialize with
.BR arbint_init ()
and release with
.BR arbint_clear ().
.TP
.B arbint_ctx_t
Execution/allocation context. A context carries the allocator used for object
storage and for exported buffers.
.TP
.B arbint_err_t
Error code enum used by mutating/extracting APIs.
.TP
.B arbint_rng_t
Mersenne Twister (MT19937) PRNG state. Initialize with
.BR arbint_rng_init ()
and release with
.BR arbint_rng_clear ().
.SH RETURN VALUES
.PP
Functions returning
.B arbint_err_t
use
.B ARBINT_OK
on success and a nonzero error code on failure.
.PP
Predicate/comparison helpers return
.B int
(typically boolean-style 0/1 or three-way compare \-1/0/+1).
.SH ERROR CODES
.TP
.B ARBINT_EINVAL
Invalid argument (for example NULL where not accepted, or invalid object state).
.TP
.B ARBINT_ENOMEM
Allocation failure.
.TP
.B ARBINT_EOVERFLOW
Value/capacity overflow for the requested conversion or operation.
.TP
.B ARBINT_ESIGN
Unsigned extraction requested from a negative value.
.TP
.B ARBINT_EDOM
Domain error (e.g.\& platform entropy source failed, or
.BR arbint_urandomm ()
bound is not positive).
.TP
.B ARBINT_EZERO
Division by zero.
.SH IMPLEMENTED API
.SS Context and Object Lifetime
.BR arbint_ctx_init_default (),
.BR arbint_ctx_init (),
.BR arbint_ctx_clear (),
.BR arbint_init (),
.BR arbint_init_all (),
.BR arbint_clear (),
.BR arbint_clear_all (),
.BR arbint_get_ctx (),
.BR arbint_set_ctx (),
.BR arbint_zero (),
.BR arbint_resize (),
.BR arbint_swap ()
.PP
These APIs initialize/destroy contexts and integer objects, rebind objects to a
new context, zero values, reserve limb capacity, and swap complete object state.
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_init_all(arbint_ctx_t *" ctx ", arbint_t " a ", ...)"
.RS
Initialize a NULL-terminated list of
.B arbint_t
values with the same context. If any initialization fails, all previously
initialized values in the list are cleared and the error is returned.
The argument list must be terminated with a
.B (arbint_t *) NULL
sentinel.
.RE
.PP
.BI "void arbint_clear_all(arbint_t " a ", ...)"
.RS
Clear a NULL-terminated list of
.B arbint_t
values. Each value is passed to
.BR arbint_clear ().
The argument list must be terminated with a
.B (arbint_t *) NULL
sentinel.
.RE
.SS Assignment and Extraction
.BR arbint_set (),
.BR arbint_set_i32 (),
.BR arbint_set_u32 (),
.BR arbint_get_i32 (),
.BR arbint_get_u32 ()
.PP
Assign from another integer or a 32-bit scalar; extract into 32-bit scalar
forms with range/sign checking.
.SS Fit Checks
.BR arbint_fits_u8 (),
.BR arbint_fits_u16 (),
.BR arbint_fits_u32 (),
.BR arbint_fits_u64 (),
.BR arbint_fits_i8 (),
.BR arbint_fits_i16 (),
.BR arbint_fits_i32 (),
.BR arbint_fits_i64 ()
.PP
Non-allocating representability predicates.
.SS Sign and Basic Predicates
.BR arbint_signum (),
.BR arbint_is_zero (),
.BR arbint_is_one (),
.BR arbint_is_neg (),
.BR arbint_is_odd (),
.BR arbint_is_even (),
.BR arbint_abs (),
.BR arbint_neg ()
.PP
Sign/shape queries and sign transforms.
.SS Comparison
.BR arbint_cmp (),
.BR arbint_cmpabs (),
.BR arbint_cmp_i32 (),
.BR arbint_cmp_u32 (),
.BR arbint_eq (),
.BR arbint_ne (),
.BR arbint_lt (),
.BR arbint_le (),
.BR arbint_gt (),
.BR arbint_ge (),
.BR arbint_eq_i32 (),
.BR arbint_ne_i32 (),
.BR arbint_lt_i32 (),
.BR arbint_le_i32 (),
.BR arbint_gt_i32 (),
.BR arbint_ge_i32 (),
.BR arbint_eq_u32 (),
.BR arbint_ne_u32 (),
.BR arbint_lt_u32 (),
.BR arbint_le_u32 (),
.BR arbint_gt_u32 (),
.BR arbint_ge_u32 ()
.PP
Three-way and predicate comparisons against big integers and 32-bit scalars.
.SS Arithmetic
.BR arbint_add (),
.BR arbint_sub (),
.BR arbint_mul (),
.BR arbint_sqr (),
.BR arbint_add_i32 (),
.BR arbint_add_u32 (),
.BR arbint_sub_i32 (),
.BR arbint_sub_u32 (),
.BR arbint_mul_i32 (),
.BR arbint_mul_u32 ()
.PP
Core signed arithmetic on arbitrary-size integers.
Scalar multiplication variants
.RB ( arbint_mul_i32 ", " arbint_mul_u32 )
provide optimized fast paths for multiplication by 32-bit values with
early-exit for special cases (0, ±1).
.SS Truncated Division and Remainder
.BR arbint_tdiv_qr (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_q (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_r (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_qr_u32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_q_u32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_r_u32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_qr_i32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_q_i32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_r_i32 ()
.PP
Truncated division obeys:
.PP
.nf
q = trunc(n / d)
r = n - q*d
.fi
.PP
with
.I |r| < |d|
and remainder sign matching the dividend (or zero).
.SS Binary Import and Export
.BR arbint_import (),
.BR arbint_export ()
.PP
Convert between
.B arbint_t
and a little-endian magnitude byte buffer.
.B arbint_export ()
exports magnitude only (no sign encoding).
.SS Hashing
.BR arbint_hash_slow (),
.BR arbint_hash_fast ()
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_hash_slow(const arbint_t " op ", uint8_t *" out_hash ", size_t " hash_len ")"
.RS
Hash the canonical value of
.I op
into
.I hash_len
bytes at
.IR out_hash .
Uses SHA-256 internally; for
.I hash_len
\(<= 32 the output is a (possibly truncated) SHA-256 digest.
For
.I hash_len
> 32 a counter-mode construction is used:
SHA-256(\fIcanonical\fP || LE32(\fIcounter\fP)) for
.I counter
= 0, 1, 2, \&... producing 32 bytes each, concatenated and truncated to
.IR hash_len .
.PP
On x86 processors with SHA-NI support, the SHA-256 compression function
is hardware-accelerated via
.BR _mm_sha256rnds2_epu32 ()
and related intrinsics.
.PP
Returns
.B ARBINT_EINVAL
if
.I op
or
.I out_hash
is NULL.
If
.I hash_len
is 0, returns
.B ARBINT_OK
without writing.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_hash_fast(const arbint_t " op ", uint32_t *" out_hash ")"
.RS
Hash the canonical value of
.I op
into a 32-bit CRC32C (Castagnoli) checksum at
.IR out_hash .
.PP
On x86 processors with SSE4.2 support, the CRC32C computation is
hardware-accelerated via
.BR _mm_crc32_u8 (),
.BR _mm_crc32_u32 (),
and
.BR _mm_crc32_u64 ()
intrinsics.
On AArch64 processors with CRC32 and PMULL support, the computation
uses
.BR __crc32cb ()
and
.BR __crc32cd ()
instructions with PMULL-based parallel stream folding.
A portable table-based fallback is used on other platforms.
.PP
Returns
.B ARBINT_EINVAL
if
.I op
or
.I out_hash
is NULL.
.RE
.PP
Both hash functions are intended for hash-table use only.
The algorithm and output are intentionally unstable across library versions
and may vary across process runs.
The canonical input is a sign byte (0x00 for zero, 0x01 for positive,
0xff for negative) followed by the magnitude in little-endian byte order
with no leading zero bytes.
.SS Shifts
.BR arbint_shl (),
.BR arbint_shr ()
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_shl(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", uint32_t " k ")"
.RS
Set
.I rop
to
.IR a " * 2^" k .
Sign is preserved.
.I rop
may alias
.IR a .
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_shr(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", uint32_t " k ")"
.RS
Set
.I rop
to
.I a
with the low
.I k
bits discarded (truncation toward zero).
Sign is preserved.
.I rop
may alias
.IR a .
.RE
.SS Bit Queries
.BR arbint_sizeinbase (),
.BR arbint_nbits (),
.BR arbint_testbit (),
.BR arbint_setbit (),
.BR arbint_clrbit (),
.BR arbint_ctz (),
.BR arbint_clz (),
.BR arbint_popcount (),
.BR arbint_hammingdist ()
.PP
.BI "size_t arbint_sizeinbase(const arbint_t " x ", int " base ")"
.RS
Return the number of digits needed to represent
.I x
in the given
.IR base ,
which must be in the range [2,\ 62].
Returns 1 if
.I x
is zero.
.RE
.PP
.BI "size_t arbint_nbits(const arbint_t " x ")"
.RS
Return the number of significant bits in
.IR x ,
i.e.\& the position of the highest set bit plus one.
Returns 0 if
.I x
is zero.
.RE
.PP
All remaining bit operations in this section use two's-complement semantics:
they behave as if operating on infinite sign-extended two's-complement
representations, then normalize the result back to the sign-magnitude
.B arbint_t
form.
Define the
.I canonical width
.RI W( x )
as the smallest positive multiple of the implementation limb width that can
represent
.I x
in two's-complement form.
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_testbit(const arbint_t " x ", size_t " bit_index ", int *" out ")"
.RS
Set
.RI * out
to 1 if bit
.I bit_index
(zero-indexed from the LSB) is set in the two's-complement representation
of
.IR x ,
or to 0 otherwise.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_setbit(arbint_t " x ", size_t " bit_index ")"
.RS
Set bit
.I bit_index
in the two's-complement representation of
.IR x .
The value is modified in place.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_clrbit(arbint_t " x ", size_t " bit_index ")"
.RS
Clear bit
.I bit_index
in the two's-complement representation of
.IR x .
The value is modified in place.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_ctz(const arbint_t " x ", size_t *" out ")"
.RS
Count the number of trailing zero bits in
.IR x .
Returns
.B ARBINT_EDOM
if
.I x
is zero.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_clz(const arbint_t " x ", size_t *" out ")"
.RS
Count the number of leading zero bits within the canonical width
.RI W( x ).
For negative values the most significant bit is always set, so
.RI * out
is 0.
Returns
.B ARBINT_EDOM
if
.I x
is zero.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_popcount(const arbint_t " x ", size_t *" out ")"
.RS
Count the number of set bits in the low
.RI W( x )
bits of
.IR x .
.PP
On x86 processors with POPCNT support, the inner loop is
hardware-accelerated via
.BR _mm_popcnt_u64 ()
intrinsics when the operand exceeds the dispatch threshold (8 limbs).
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_hammingdist(const arbint_t " a ", const arbint_t " b ", size_t *" out ")"
.RS
Count the number of bit positions where
.I a
and
.I b
differ within the low
.RI max(W( a ),\ W( b ))
bits.
.PP
Hardware POPCNT acceleration is used when available, as with
.BR arbint_popcount ().
.RE
.SS Bitwise Logic
.BR arbint_or (),
.BR arbint_and (),
.BR arbint_xor (),
.BR arbint_not (),
.BR arbint_and_u32 (),
.BR arbint_and_i32 (),
.BR arbint_or_u32 (),
.BR arbint_or_i32 (),
.BR arbint_xor_u32 (),
.BR arbint_xor_i32 ()
.PP
All bitwise logic operations use two's-complement semantics as described
in
.BR "Bit Queries" .
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_or(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", const arbint_t " b ")"
.RS
Set
.I rop
to
.IR a " | " b
(bitwise OR).
.I rop
may alias either input.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_and(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", const arbint_t " b ")"
.RS
Set
.I rop
to
.IR a " & " b
(bitwise AND).
.I rop
may alias either input.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_xor(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", const arbint_t " b ")"
.RS
Set
.I rop
to
.IR a " ^ " b
(bitwise exclusive OR).
.I rop
may alias either input.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_not(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ")"
.RS
Set
.I rop
to the two's-complement bitwise NOT of
.IR a ,
which is equivalent to
.RI \-( a +1).
.I rop
may alias
.IR a .
.RE
.PP
The immediate-operand variants perform the same operation with a 32-bit
scalar second operand.
Signed
.RB ( _i32 )
variants sign-extend the scalar to match the width of
.IR a ;
unsigned
.RB ( _u32 )
variants zero-extend.
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_and_u32(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", uint32_t " b ")"
.br
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_and_i32(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", int32_t " b ")"
.br
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_or_u32(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", uint32_t " b ")"
.br
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_or_i32(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", int32_t " b ")"
.br
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_xor_u32(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", uint32_t " b ")"
.br
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_xor_i32(arbint_t " rop ", const arbint_t " a ", int32_t " b ")"
.SS Random Number Generation
.BR arbint_rng_init (),
.BR arbint_rng_clear (),
.BR arbint_urandomb (),
.BR arbint_urandomm ()
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_rng_init(arbint_rng_t *" rng ", const void *" seed ", size_t " seed_len ")"
.RS
Initialize an MT19937 PRNG state. If
.I seed
is NULL, the state is seeded from a platform entropy source (see
.B ENTROPY SOURCES
below). If
.I seed
is non-NULL, it must point to a buffer of
.I seed_len
bytes, where
.I seed_len
is a positive multiple of 4.
Returns
.B ARBINT_EINVAL
if no platform entropy source is available (when
.I seed
is NULL) or if
.I seed_len
is invalid.
Returns
.B ARBINT_EDOM
if the platform entropy source fails at runtime.
.RE
.PP
.BI "void arbint_rng_clear(arbint_rng_t *" rng ")"
.RS
Securely zero the PRNG state to prevent leaking generated sequences.
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_urandomb(arbint_t " rop ", arbint_rng_t *" rng ", size_t " k ")"
.RS
Set
.I rop
to a uniformly random non-negative integer in [0,\ 2^\fIk\fP).
.RE
.PP
.BI "arbint_err_t arbint_urandomm(arbint_t " rop ", arbint_rng_t *" rng ", const arbint_t " bound ")"
.RS
Set
.I rop
to a uniformly random non-negative integer in [0,\ \fIbound\fP).
Uses rejection sampling to eliminate modulo bias.
.I rop
may alias
.IR bound .
Returns
.B ARBINT_EDOM
if
.I bound
\(<= 0 or if rejection sampling exhausts 256 attempts.
.RE
.SH ALIASING RULES
.PP
For single-output operations, destination may alias an input.
.PP
For two-output division APIs, output aliases are accepted; if both outputs are
the same object, the last-written output value remains in that object.
.SH MEMORY NOTES
.PP
.B arbint_export ()
allocates the output buffer using the allocator in the source object's context.
The caller owns that buffer and must free it with the same allocator contract.
.SH ALGORITHMIC NOTES
.PP
.B Multiplication
dispatches between a generic implementation and an optimized
BMI2 path on supported x86 targets (using runtime CPU detection).
The implementation switches between schoolbook (O(n²)) and Karatsuba
(O(n^log₂3)) algorithms based on operand size (threshold: 32 limbs).
.PP
.B Division
by multi-limb divisors uses binary long division with magnitude comparison.
Division by single-limb divisors (u32/i32 variants) uses a reciprocal-based
algorithm (Granlund-Montgomery technique) that replaces expensive hardware
DIV instructions with two multiplications per limb plus minimal ALU operations.
This provides significant speedup for the common case of dividing large
integers by small constants.
.PP
.B Doubling
operations automatically select an AVX2-optimized SIMD implementation when
available and operand size exceeds the threshold (16 limbs), providing
1.5-2.5x speedup on modern x86-64 processors.
.PP
All division variants apply appropriate sign rules for truncation semantics
after magnitude computation.
.PP
.B Hashing
uses a self-contained SHA-256 implementation (FIPS 180-4) for
.BR arbint_hash_slow ()
and CRC32C (Castagnoli polynomial) for
.BR arbint_hash_fast ().
Both dispatch to hardware-accelerated implementations when available:
SHA-NI extensions for SHA-256 compression and SSE4.2 intrinsics for CRC32C.
Canonical data is fed through a streaming interface with O(1) auxiliary memory.
.PP
.B Bitwise operations
use two's-complement sign decomposition: each binary operation branches on the
four possible sign combinations of its operands, converts negative magnitudes
to two's-complement form, performs the limb-wise operation, and re-normalizes.
.BR arbint_popcount ()
and
.BR arbint_hammingdist ()
dispatch between a portable scalar loop and a POPCNT-accelerated
implementation on x86 processors with hardware POPCNT support
(dispatch threshold: 8 limbs).
.PP
.B arbint_sizeinbase ()
uses a fixed-point reciprocal lookup table to avoid floating-point arithmetic,
computing
.RI ceil( nbits " * " recip )
where
.I recip
approximates 1/log2(\fIbase\fP).
.SH ENTROPY SOURCES
.PP
When
.BR arbint_rng_init ()
is called with a NULL seed, it reads entropy from the best available
platform source. Sources are selected at compile time in the following
priority order:
.PP
.TP
.B Windows
.BR BCryptGenRandom ()
(requires
.IR bcrypt.h ,
Vista+), then
.BR CryptGenRandom ()
(requires
.IR wincrypt.h ,
XP+), then
.B RtlGenRandom
(loaded dynamically from
.IR advapi32.dll ,
XP+; needs only
.IR windows.h ).
.TP
.B macOS/BSD
.BR arc4random_buf ()
(kernel-seeded, cannot fail).
.TP
.B Linux/POSIX
.I /dev/urandom
(opened, read, and closed on each call).
.PP
If no platform entropy source was detected at configure time, or if entropy
support was disabled with
.BR \-\-disable-entropy ,
then NULL-seed initialization returns
.BR ARBINT_EINVAL .
Callers must then supply their own seed buffer.
.SH CONFIGURE OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-disable\-entropy
Do not compile any platform entropy source.
.BR arbint_rng_init ()
with a NULL seed will always return
.BR ARBINT_EINVAL ;
callers must provide their own seed.
The PRNG itself
.RB ( arbint_urandomb ", " arbint_urandomm )
is unaffected.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <arbint.h>

arbint_ctx_t ctx;
arbint_t a, b, q, r;

arbint_ctx_init_default(&ctx);
arbint_init_all(&ctx, a, b, q, r, (arbint_t *) NULL);

arbint_set_i32(a, -17);
arbint_set_i32(b, 5);
arbint_tdiv_qr(q, r, a, b); /* q = -3, r = -2 */

arbint_clear_all(a, b, q, r, (arbint_t *) NULL);
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkg-config (1)
